# 4. `getText()`

This is where beginners commonly get confused.

Use `getText()` to retrieve **visible text displayed inside an element**.

Example HTML:

```html
<h1>Student Registration Form</h1>
```

```java
WebElement heading =
        driver.findElement(By.tagName("h1"));

String text = heading.getText();

System.out.println(text);
```

Output:

```text
Student Registration Form
```

Another example:

```html
<button>Submit</button>
```

```java
String text = submitButton.getText();
```

returns:

```text
Submit
```

### But for an input:

```html
<input id="firstName" value="Jaswanth">
```

Don't normally use:

```java
firstName.getText(); // ❌
```

Use:

```java
firstName.getAttribute("value"); // ✅
```

---

# 5. `getAttribute()`

This is used when you want an element's **HTML attribute/property value**.

Example:

```html
<input
    id="firstName"
    placeholder="First Name"
    value="Jaswanth">
```

You can retrieve:

```java
firstName.getAttribute("id");
```

